/* book Worked example 2: Q: Ammonia synthesis, N₂ + 3H₂ ⇌ 2NH₃, has K = 5.81 × 10⁵ at 298.15 K and ΔH°₂₉₈ = 2(−46.11) = −92.22 kJ/mol. Estimate K at 500 K with ΔH° taken as constant, and compare with the value from a full heat-capacity calculation, K = 0.100.
   1. ln K₁ = ln(5.81 × 10⁵) = 13.272 at T₁ = 298.15 K.
   2. ΔH°/R = −92 220/8.314 = −11 092 K. 1/T₂ − 1/T₁ = 1/500 − 1/298.15 = −0.0013540 K⁻¹.
   3. ln(K₂/K₁) = −(ΔH°/R)(1/T₂ − 1/T₁) = −(−11 092)(−0.0013540) = −15.019.
   4. ln K₂ = 13.272 − 15.019 = −1.747, so K₂ = 0.174.
   5. With ΔCp° included, ΔH° grows from −92.2 kJ/mol at 298 K to about −100.0 kJ/mol at 500 K, so K falls further: the full calculation gives ln K = −2.302 and K = 0.100. The constant-ΔH° estimate is 74 % high over this 200 K span.
   Answer: K(500 K) ≈ 0.174 with constant ΔH°, against 0.100 when ΔCp° is included: K falls by a factor of about 6 × 10⁶ between 298 and 500 K because the reaction is exothermic. */
